# stateful: the register keeps its value from one packet to the next.
#
# Our own vectors; p4c's never hit the same cell twice. The file replays on a
# fresh load, so r is all zero at the first packet, and the state then
# carries across every packet below: the harness keeps one Loaded per file.
#
# Per packet, with idx = reg_idx_to_update and v = value_to_add:
#
#   ingress half:  pkts.count(idx); x = r[idx]; if (x == 0) r[idx] = 0x2a;
#   egress half:   tmp = r[idx] + v;  r[idx] = tmp;  debug byte = tmp
#
# so a cell is seeded with 0x2a the first time it is read as zero and
# accumulates afterwards; the output's third byte is the cell's new value.
#
# Packet 1, idx 0, v 0x17:  r[0] = 0x00 -> seeded 0x2a
#                           tmp = 0x2a + 0x17 = 0x41;  r[0] = 0x41
# Packet 2, idx 0, v 0x17:  r[0] = 0x41, not seeded: the previous packet's
#                           write is what this one reads
#                           tmp = 0x41 + 0x17 = 0x58;  r[0] = 0x58
# Packet 3, idx 1, v 0x05:  another cell, untouched: r[1] = 0x00 -> 0x2a
#                           tmp = 0x2a + 0x05 = 0x2f;  r[1] = 0x2f
# Packet 4, idx 0, v 0xa8:  r[0] = 0x58
#                           tmp = 0x58 + 0xa8 = 0x100, wraps to 0x00 in
#                           bit<8>;  r[0] = 0x00
# Packet 5, idx 0, v 0x01:  r[0] = 0x00 again, so the seed guard fires a
#                           second time: a cell that wraps to zero is
#                           indistinguishable from a fresh one
#                           tmp = 0x2a + 0x01 = 0x2b;  r[0] = 0x2b
# Packet 6, idx 1, v 0x01:  r[1] = 0x2f, untouched since packet 3; the input
#                           debug byte is 0xff to show it is overwritten
#                           tmp = 0x2f + 0x01 = 0x30;  r[1] = 0x30
#
# The counter pkts is bumped at idx on every packet (pkts[0] = 4, pkts[1] =
# 2 at the end) but STF has no way to read it; it is here so the printer
# and the oracle see a counter.
#
# `$` demands the exact length: the output is the three header bytes and
# nothing else, since the parser consumed the whole packet.

packet 0 00 17 00
expect 0 00 17 41 $

packet 0 00 17 00
expect 0 00 17 58 $

packet 0 01 05 00
expect 0 01 05 2f $

packet 0 00 a8 00
expect 0 00 a8 00 $

packet 0 00 01 00
expect 0 00 01 2b $

packet 0 01 01 ff
expect 0 01 01 30 $
